@page "/docs/extensions/reporting/data-sources"

<Seo Canonical="/docs/extensions/reporting/data-sources" Title="Blazorise Reporting data sources" Description="Bind Blazorise reports to objects, DataSets, DataTables, and CSV sources." />

<DocsPageTitle Path="Extensions/Reporting/Data Sources" AddedVersion="New">
    Reporting data sources
</DocsPageTitle>

<DocsPageLead>
    Reports can bind to an object model, DataSet, DataTable, or CSV source, expose fields in the field explorer, and repeat detail bands from enumerable data paths.
</DocsPageLead>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Object data source">
        Object data sources expose scalar properties, nested objects, and collections to the designer.
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="ReportingDataSourcesExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <ReportingDataSourcesExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="DataSet data source">
        Use <Code>ReportDataSetDataSource</Code> to bind reports to enterprise data sources that expose <Code>DataSet</Code> or <Code>DataTable</Code> objects. Set <Code>TableName</Code> to bind one table directly, or omit it to expose tables as nested data sources.
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="ReportingDataSetDataSourceExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <ReportingDataSetDataSourceExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSubtitle>
    CSV data source
</DocsPageSubtitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Install the <Code>Blazorise.Reporting.DataSources.Csv</Code> package and import the <Code>Blazorise.Reporting.DataSources.Csv</Code> namespace. Register the provider after the core Reporting services. Calling <Code>AddBlazoriseReportingCsvDataSource()</Code> without callbacks uses the default HTTP configuration and a 5 MB source limit.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Registration">
        The optional callbacks configure the HTTP client and decide which remote CSV addresses the application permits.
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionSource Code="ReportingCsvDataSourceRegistrationExample" />
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Set <Code>ReportCsvDataSource.Source</Code> to inline CSV text, a local file path in a server application, or an absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL. The first row supplies field names by default, and the provider infers the field types from the remaining rows.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Declarative CSV">
        Give the source a name and use that name as the data source of a detail band.
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionSource Code="ReportingCsvDataSourceExample" />
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    HTTP sources are downloaded by the browser in Blazor WebAssembly and therefore follow browser CORS rules. In server applications they are downloaded by the server, so use <Code>ResourceAllowed</Code> to restrict user-controlled URLs. Server registrations disable and reject redirects by default. If the primary HTTP handler is replaced, keep automatic redirects disabled so an allowed address cannot redirect to a blocked destination.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Detail data source">
        Assign the detail band data source to a collection path. Fields inside the detail band are then resolved against each collection item.
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="ReportingDetailDataSourceExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <ReportingDetailDataSourceExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

